// Mid-circuit measurement: collapse, and what `reset` really does.
//
// Concept
//   A measurement is not a passive read. It collapses the state, and everything
//   after it evolves from the collapsed state. This circuit measures, resets,
//   and measures again, so the two classical bits are *independent* coin flips
//   rather than correlated ones.
//
//   `reset` is a measurement whose result is discarded, followed by a flip if
//   the result was 1. It is not a projection onto |0>, and the difference
//   matters for an entangled qubit.
//
// Expected quantum behaviour
//   After the first H and measure, the qubit is definitely 0 or 1. `reset`
//   returns it to |0> regardless. The second H then produces a fresh 50/50
//   superposition that knows nothing about the first outcome.
//
// Expected measurement results
//   All four of 00, 01, 10 and 11 occur, each about 25% of shots. Because this
//   circuit has a mid-circuit measurement, the simulator replays it once per
//   shot rather than sampling from a single final statevector - `qforge run`
//   prints which strategy it used.
//
// Mathematical interpretation
//   The joint distribution factorises as P(c0, c1) = P(c0) * P(c1) with each
//   marginal uniform, precisely because the reset erased the correlation. Drop
//   the `reset` and the second measurement would instead agree with the first
//   every time, since measurement is repeatable.
//
// @expect qubits=1 clbits=2 gates=2
// @expect-support 00,01,10,11
// @expect-frequency 00=0.25
// @expect-frequency 11=0.25

qubit q;
bit[2] c;

h q;
measure q -> c[0];

reset q;

h q;
measure q -> c[1];
